WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.560
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Lan.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.150
LAN TRAN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.990
MARTIN OMANDER: What
are we building today?

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.980
LAN TRAN: So I need to automate
some Admin tasks in Looker,

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.100
which is Google Cloud's business
intelligence and big data

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.570
analytics platform.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.990
I've heard that it can be
done with Cloud Functions.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.550
MARTIN OMANDER: That's right,
but I have to warn you,

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.785
I don't know Looker at all.

00:00:20.785 --> 00:00:21.660
LAN TRAN: No worries.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.760
If you bring your Cloud
Functions know-how,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.413
I will bring my Looker skills.

00:00:25.413 --> 00:00:26.580
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds good.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.540
Let's do it.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.760
LAN TRAN: So I often need to
register Looker user accounts

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.670
for my coworkers, but
sometimes I forget

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:44.960
and people get annoyed.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.870
So I would like to
automate that process.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.490
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, got it.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.670
LAN TRAN: So my users usually
fill out a Google form

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.820
so that email addresses end
up in a Google spreadsheet.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.800
Can we build a
Cloud Functions so

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.070
that it reads that spreadsheet
nicely and then creates Looker

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:04.420
user accounts for my coworkers?

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, we can.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.890
This is actually pretty common
use case for Cloud Functions.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.760
Run on a schedule, read a Sheet
or some other data source,

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.402
and take actions
based on that data.

00:01:15.402 --> 00:01:17.860
LAN TRAN: Sounds like Cloud
Functions is a glue [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.300
in the Cloud.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.420
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, that's
a good way of putting it.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.410
Cloud Functions are great for
gluing together other systems.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.020
LAN TRAN: So I look at
Cloud Function Documentation

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.203
and I saw that we can write
the functions in Python.

00:01:32.203 --> 00:01:33.370
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, we can.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.870
Google Cloud Functions also
support node, go, Java, .NET,

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.915
Ruby, and PHP.

00:01:39.915 --> 00:01:40.540
LAN TRAN: Good.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.580
I know Python
pretty well, but how

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.412
do you read Google
Sheet in Python?

00:01:45.412 --> 00:01:46.870
MARTIN OMANDER:
Here is an example.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.090
First, it gets the
default credentials,

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.240
then it creates a service
object, then a Sheet API

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.050
client.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.350
And we can call values.get
on that Sheet API client

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.390
and passing the Sheet ID
and range of cells to read.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.790
But now it's my turn
to ask you a question.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:08.470
How do you create Looker
accounts from Python?

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.250
LAN TRAN: It's pretty
simple, actually.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.360
So we need to import the
Looker SDK like this.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.200
We create an SDK object and
then we can call methods on it,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.400
like the right user to
create new user account.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.810
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.770
So I will go to my project
in Google Cloud Console.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:30.640
And here I'll select
Cloud Functions.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.910
I'll wait for it to be enabled.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.540
And then I click here to
create a new function.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.750
I give the new
function a good name.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.240
And I'll make sure that
Require authentication has

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.120
been selected, so that it can't
be triggered by just anyone

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.170
out there on the internet.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:52.990
And when I click Next here,
the Code Editor opens up.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.140
Good.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.700
So we can wrap these code
fragments in Python functions,

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.970
then we just need to tell
Google which of these functions

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:02.530
is the entry point.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.770
That entry point should take
a flask or request object

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.273
as its only input parameter.

00:03:08.273 --> 00:03:09.940
LAN TRAN: I think
between the two of us,

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:12.340
we know enough to
write this code.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.960
MARTIN OMANDER: Let's do it.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.140
First, I'll write the imports.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.120
And the first two
will be needed to read

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.515
the Google Sheet and the next
two import the Looker SDK.

00:03:24.515 --> 00:03:26.140
LAN TRAN: And I will
write the function

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.670
that is the entry point.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.370
It calls a function that
reads the email addresses

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.920
from a Google Sheet,
looks over these emails,

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.940
calls a function that creates
a Looker user account for each

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.887
of these email.

00:03:38.887 --> 00:03:39.970
MARTIN OMANDER: Very good.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.920
Then I will write
the function here

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.650
that reads email
addresses from the Sheet.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:48.640
It will create a Sheet client
and call values.get on it,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.150
and read all the values
from the B column.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.800
I will open our
Google Sheet also

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.840
and copy the Sheet
ID from that address

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.090
and paste it into the Sheet
ID variable in our code.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.430
LAN TRAN: Then I will write
the function that creates

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.320
user's account in Looker.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.950
First, it calls create
user on a Looker client,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.290
then it calls create
user credentials email

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.350
to set up new email for
the user's credentials.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:21.130
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:26.080
And I'll also put in our
requirements.txt file.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:27.070
All right.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.490
But Lan, how does
Looker know who we are

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:34.360
and what Looker project
we're adding users to?

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.080
LAN TRAN: So Looker will read
the base URL, the client ID,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.360
and a secret from any file.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.070
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, got it.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:43.870
OK, so we put it in there.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.560
And I'll deploy the function
by clicking this button.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.090
LAN TRAN: Let's
test the function.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.960
MARTIN OMANDER: I'll
click here to call it.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.960
Oh, that doesn't look good.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.190
Let's check the log.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.940
Something about permissions.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.610
LAN TRAN: We never granted
access to the spreadsheet,

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:06.315
did we?

00:05:06.315 --> 00:05:09.330
If I click Details, it
looks like the Functions is

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.920
running as [INAUDIBLE] account.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:16.500
So I copied that, I go to the
spreadsheet, I click Share,

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.530
then I grant the viewer
access to the spreadsheet.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.930
Click that test button again.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.320
MARTIN OMANDER: All
right, here it goes.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.480
Oh, looks like it works.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.760
And the log, oh yeah,
the log says here

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.740
that the accounts were created.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.360
LAN TRAN: But Martin,
there's one part

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.700
you have forgotten about.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.290
MARTIN OMANDER:
Didn't we do it all?

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:38.100
LAN TRAN: No.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.040
We have not scheduled
it to run nightly.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.600
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh, you're right.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.780
Let's do that in
Cloud Scheduler.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.990
First, I'll copy the URL
that triggers this function.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:53.320
Then I go to Cloud Scheduler
and click this button

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.730
to schedule a job.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.430
I give a little name here.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:03.380
I'll enter 00***, which
means every night at midnight

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.110
in the UNIX cron format.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:11.120
Then I'll set it to your time
zone, Lan, Japan Standard Time.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.370
I will set the
Target Type to HTTP,

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.967
and paste in the URL
for our Cloud Function.

00:06:16.967 --> 00:06:18.550
And because the
function isn't public,

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:22.960
I will need to set Auth
Header to OIDC token.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.310
I'll pick the default
compute account for now,

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:27.010
but if we take
this to production,

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.290
we should consider
creating a custom service

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:31.690
account that has
access to trigger Cloud

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.490
Functions and nothing more.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:34.150
LAN TRAN: Yeah.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.310
That's the principle
of the list privilege.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.170
I like that.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:39.640
MARTIN OMANDER: Yes, exactly.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.015
LAN TRAN: Let's
test it right now

00:06:41.015 --> 00:06:44.610
so we don't have to wait until
tomorrow to see if it works.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.200
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, good idea.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.710
Let's click this Run button
and give it a minute.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.240
OK, I'll click View Logs.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:54.590
Looks like it ran.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:57.950
And if I remove all
these filters here,

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.853
we see the logs from
our Cloud Function too.

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:01.520
LAN TRAN: Great.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.480
It's working.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.600
Now I can just tell my users
to submit their email addresses

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.730
in my phone and Looker
accounts will be

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.840
created for them at midnight.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Very good.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.960
Thank you everyone for watching.

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.620
If you have questions for
Lan or me about this episode,

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.690
or feedback, or ideas
for future episodes,

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:21.050
please enter them in
the comments below.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:21.650
Bye.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:25.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

